===
Group Order Session Decisions
=============================

Shareable room
--------------
- Each session gets a URL-safe, cryptographically random public ID with roughly
  144 bits of randomness.
- The share link is /group-order/<session_id>. Anyone with this hard-to-guess
  link can view the room and add orders while it is open; no account is needed.
- Creating a room also returns a separate organizer token. Only its SHA-256
  hash is persisted.

Persistence
-----------
- Sessions are stored as one JSON file per room under .group-orders/.
- Writes use a temporary file followed by an atomic replace.
- Per-room locks prevent concurrent requests in the threaded server from
  overwriting one another.
- This lightweight store is intended for the current single-process server. A
  multi-process deployment should use a transactional database.

Orders and aggregation
----------------------
- Each submitted drink is stored as an order line containing the person's
  display name, drink, modifiers, quantity, notes, and timestamps.
- Room reads return every order line plus an aggregate summary: order-line
  count, total drink quantity, distinct person count, and totals by person.
- Names are grouped case-insensitively for aggregation, while the first entered
  spelling is retained for display.
- A room accepts at most 200 order lines. Quantities are limited to 1-20, and
  text fields have bounded lengths.

Associating orders with people
------------------------------
- Every order stores the contributor's display name and a random public
  participant ID.
- Every new order also returns a separate private edit token. Its hash is
  stored with the order; the raw token is never returned by later room reads.
- The edit token allows that contributor to edit or remove the order while the
  room is open. The organizer token can manage any order.
- Display names are labels, not authentication. Possession of the edit token
  is the proof that someone may change an order.

Lifecycle
---------
- Sessions start open and accept additions, edits, and removals.
- They expire after 24 hours by default. The creator may choose a positive
  duration up to seven days.
- The organizer may lock a room, making it read-only, and may reopen it later.
- The organizer may close a room permanently. Closed rooms cannot be reopened.
- Expiration is also terminal. Expired rooms remain readable during the
  retention period but reject writes.
- Closed and expired rooms remain available for review. Rooms more than seven
  days past expiration are deleted, and at most 200 recent closed/expired room
  files are retained. Live rooms are never removed by the archive-size cap.

Organizer dashboard and live updates
------------------------------------
- The organizer view is /group-order/<session_id>/organizer. Its private token
  may arrive once in the URL fragment, which browsers do not send to the
  server; the page moves it to localStorage and immediately removes the
  fragment from the address bar. Later organizer reads and mutations send the
  token in X-Organizer-Token. The participant URL never contains this token.
- The dashboard polls its authenticated room endpoint every five seconds while
  the tab is visible and refreshes immediately when a hidden tab becomes
  visible again. Five seconds is live enough for a small group order, avoids a
  WebSocket/SSE server and reconnect protocol in this standard-library app,
  and bounds idle traffic by pausing background-tab polls. A manual refresh is
  also available.
- The dashboard shows running people, drink, and order-line totals. Lines are
  grouped case-insensitively by person and a separate rollup totals quantities
  by drink. Exact same-person/order configurations are marked as possible
  duplicates, but are never removed automatically.
- Pausing a room blocks participant additions, edits, and removals. The
  organizer may still remove a line while paused, making it possible to freeze
  the incoming set before moderating junk or duplicates.

Finalization and handoff
------------------------
- Finalization is an organizer-authenticated, idempotent operation. Under the
  room lock it snapshots the current lines, runs them through the same importer
  normalization used by spreadsheet orders, saves a normal pipeline run, and
  permanently closes the room. Repeating the request returns the same run while
  it is retained instead of creating a competing cart review.
- The response points to /preview/<run_id>. From that point the existing Task 5
  preview, matching, cart build, manifest, and Kung Fu Tea handoff are reused;
  the group-order path never submits checkout or payment itself.
- Empty or expired rooms cannot be finalized. A room manually closed through
  the older lifecycle endpoint can still be pushed to preview, so closing it
  early does not strand valid submissions.
- Finalized run URLs are returned only by the authenticated organizer endpoint,
  not by the public participant room response.

Store selection
---------------
- The organizer chooses a visible takeout location from Kung Fu Tea's full
  store directory before creating a room. The selected restaurant id and
  display name are immutable room fields.
- Participants load the menu for that room's restaurant id. Finalization copies
  the same id into the saved run, so option normalization, preview matching,
  live-menu refresh, cart creation, and the Kung Fu Tea handoff cannot silently
  switch locations.
- `GET /api/stores` caches the live directory for six hours. Creating a room
  fetches and normalizes an uncached selected menu into `.menu-cache/`; bundled
  snapshots are offline fallbacks. A room cannot be created for an unknown id,
  and browser-supplied store names are never trusted.

Per-person budgets
------------------
- The optional budget is a hard cap on one person's combined priced lines,
  including quantities and priced modifiers. It is not a separate allowance
  for every drink.
- The room's captured menu is authoritative; browser-supplied prices are never
  accepted. Lines that cannot be priced are shown but exempt from the cap.
- Lowering a cap keeps existing lines. A participant may keep or reduce a
  grandfathered total but cannot increase it; organizer moderation may still
  override a line. Closed rooms cannot change the cap.
