User wants:
1. Change "Which way do I need?" to "Which way should I use?"
2. Each option under it should link to the group order or import spreadsheet section (i.e., anchor links that open/scroll to the relevant path panel).

Looking at index.html structure: the onboarding chooser is:
```html
<div class="onboarding-chooser">
  <p><strong>Which way do I need?</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Already have everyone's drinks in a spreadsheet?</strong> → Import a spreadsheet.</li>
    <li><strong>Still collecting?</strong> → Start a group-order link and share it.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
```

The two path panels: `#group-path` and `#sheet-path` divs, toggled by `selectPath('group'|'sheet')` in app.js. The chooser buttons are `#choose-group` and `#choose-sheet`.

Best implementation: make the chooser items actual links/buttons that call selectPath + scroll to the panel. Options:
- Use `<a href="#group-path">` etc. — but panels are hidden until selected, so a plain anchor won't work; need JS to select the path first.
- Add buttons in the chooser that call `selectPath('sheet')` / `selectPath('group')` and scroll into view + focus.

Simplest robust approach consistent with codebase: turn the "→ Import a spreadsheet" / "→ Start a group-order link" text into `<button class="text-button">` elements with ids, wire click handlers in app.js to selectPath + scroll to panel + focus heading.

Also enhance selectPath to scroll? No — keep selectPath as-is; add scrolling in the new handlers.

Implementation:

index.html:
```html
<div class="onboarding-chooser">
  <p><strong>Which way should I use?</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Already have everyone's drinks in a spreadsheet?</strong> → <button class="text-button" id="onboarding-go-sheet" type="button">Import a spreadsheet</button>.</li>
    <li><strong>Still collecting?</strong> → <button class="text-button" id="onboarding-go-group" type="button">Start a group-order link</button> and share it.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
```

app.js: wire:
```js
function goToPath(which, targetId) {
  selectPath(which);
  const panel = $(targetId);
  if (panel) {
    if (panel.scrollIntoView) {
      try { panel.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' }); } catch (error) { panel.scrollIntoView(); }
    }
    const heading = panel.querySelector('h2');
    ...
  }
}
```

Hmm, focus: for group path, focusing store search might pop things; better to focus the h2 with tabindex -1 like the dismiss handler does. Actually simpler: scroll into view and move focus to the panel heading for keyboard/screen-reader users. Let me write:

```js
if ($('onboarding-go-sheet')) $('onboarding-go-sheet').addEventListener('click', () => goToPath('sheet', 'sheet-path'));
if ($('onboarding-go-group')) $('onboarding-go-group').addEventListener('click', () => goToPath('group', 'group-path'));

function goToPath(which, panelId) {
  selectPath(which);
  const panel = $(panelId);
  if (!panel) return;
  if (panel.scrollIntoView) {
    try { panel.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' }); }
    catch (error) { panel.scrollIntoView(); }
  }
  const heading = panel.querySelector('h2');
  if (heading && heading.focus) {
    try { heading.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1'); heading.focus({ preventScroll: true }); }
    catch (error) { /* visual only */ }
  }
}
```

Note: app.js's `$` helper is getElementById, and `panel.querySelector` — real browsers fine. Note function declaration order: handlers reference goToPath defined below — function declarations hoist, fine.

